.TH "UBERTOOTH\-AFH" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-afh\fR \- passive detection of AFH channel map
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-afh \-u <uap> \-l <lap> [\-r] [\-m <count>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Classic Bluetooth piconets commonly make use of Adaptive Frequency Hopping (AFH) to avoid interference with other signals in 2\.4 GHz\. This tool attempts to determine the AFH map being used for a given piconet identified by UAP and LAP\.
.P
At a minimum you must provide UAP and LAP\. See ubertooth\-rx(1) for a method for sniffing these over the air and details on where else to find them\. By default the tool will only print the AFH map when it is updated\. By providing the \fB\-r\fR flag, the AFH map will be printed out in binary form once per second\.
.P
The capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-l <LAP>\fR
LAP of target piconet (3 bytes / 6 hex digits)
.TP
\fB\-u <UAP>\fR
UAP of target piconet (1 byte / 2 hex digits)
.TP
\fB\-m <int>\fR
threshold for channel removal: a channel is marked unused once this many packets arrive without it being seen; 1 or more (default: 5)\. It counts packets, not seconds, and the default of 5 is at the low end; see THE REMOVAL THRESHOLD
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
print AFH channel map once every second (default: print on update)
.P
Other options:
.TP
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR
timeout for initial AFH map detection (not required); see DETECTION AND MONITORING\. No effect with \fB\-r\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-e <0\-5>\fR
maximum access code errors (default: 2, range: 0\-5)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "DETECTION AND MONITORING"
\fBubertooth\-afh\fR does not stop on its own\. It keeps monitoring the piconet and reporting changes to the map until it is interrupted (Ctrl\-C), both with and without \fB\-t\fR\.
.P
While it listens, the board hops across the channels the piconet has not been seen on, and the tool tells the board to stop visiting a channel once a packet from the piconet has arrived there\. That is how the map fills in: a channel is reported used when a packet is received on it\.
.P
The one exception is a single unused channel between two used ones\. The tool guesses that such a channel is one no packet has landed on yet rather than one the piconet left out, so when a packet arrives on a channel whose next\-but\-one neighbor is already used, the channel between them is marked used too, with a note on standard error:
.IP "" 4
.nf
note: activating additional channel 37
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing in the specification stops a piconet from leaving out one channel on its own, so a map reported by this tool can name a channel the piconet does not use\. Only a packet is evidence\.
.P
Basic Rate has 79 channels, 0 to 78, and the rule needs a real channel on the far side of the gap, so it never reaches past either end of the band: channels 0 and 78 are reported used only once a packet arrives on them\. Channel 1 is still filled by a packet on channel 0 when channel 2 is used, and channel 77 by a packet on channel 78 when channel 76 is\.
.P
The map lives on the board only while this run does\. Every mode start resets the board's hop mode and AFH map, so this run begins from an empty map, and the next capture \(em another \fBubertooth\-afh\fR, an \fBubertooth\-rx\fR, anything \(em hops across the whole channel set rather than on the map found here\. That holds even if this run is killed and leaves the board streaming\. See ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS\.
.P
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR only adds an initial detection phase: the tool listens for that many seconds, prints the map it found, and then starts monitoring\. Only the timeout moves it on to monitoring: interrupting the detection phase (Ctrl\-C) ends the run there, printing the map detected so far, rather than starting to monitor something you have just asked the tool to stop\. If that phase received no packet from the piconet there is no map to print, so the tool says so, does not start monitoring, and exits with status 2 instead of printing an all\-zero map (which reads as a detected map whose piconet uses no channel at all):
.IP "" 4
.nf
$ ubertooth\-afh \-l 446688 \-u 22 \-t 3
error: no packets from ??:??:22:44:66:88 within 3 s; AFH map unknown, not monitoring
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing is wrong with the board in that case: the piconet is out of range, it is not transmitting, or the UAP/LAP is not the one in use\. A longer \fB\-t\fR, or no \fB\-t\fR at all, keeps listening\.
.P
To wait for a piconet that is not in range yet (start the tool, then bring the devices close or start the connection), run without \fB\-t\fR: monitoring starts at once, waits as long as it takes, and notes \fB+ channel N is used now\fR on standard error as the piconet's packets arrive\. \fB\-t\fR is for a bounded check that gives up when nothing is heard in time\.
.P
\fB\-r\fR has its own loop, which prints the map once a second from the start and ignores \fB\-t\fR\.
.SH "THE REMOVAL THRESHOLD"
\fB\-m\fR counts packets, not seconds: a channel leaves the map once that many packets from the piconet have arrived with none of them on that channel\. So what the threshold means depends on how many channels the piconet is using, not on how long the run has been going\.
.P
That is a trade\-off, and both ends of it cost something\. A small threshold notices quickly when the piconet really does stop using a channel, and reports channels it is still using as dropped\. A large one settles into a map that stops changing, and is slow to notice a channel the piconet has given up\.
.P
The default of 5 is at the small end, far enough that the map does not settle at all for a piconet on the whole band\. A piconet hops to a different channel each slot, so with 79 channels in use a given channel carries about one packet in 79: five packets almost never include it, and the monitor output is a stream of \fB+ channel N is used now\fR and \fB\- channel N is not used any more\fR lines rather than a map that holds still\. A threshold has to be a good few times the number of channels in use before a used channel is reliably seen again within it, which puts a settling value for the whole band in the hundreds\.
.P
That arithmetic is the shape of it, not a measurement\. The default is still 5 because no measured value has replaced it: choosing one means running against a real piconet whose map is known, and seeing both how long a map takes to settle and how quickly a channel the piconet drops is noticed\. Until then, raise \fB\-m\fR yourself if the map matters more than the reaction time\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-afh\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: timeout must be a whole number of seconds, 0 for none (got abc)\fR\.
.P
Both halves of the piconet address are required, and a command line missing either is refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: \-l <LAP> and \-u <UAP> are both required: the map is detected for one piconet\fR\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries the detected channel maps\. Errors, warnings and the tool's own progress go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR\. The channel\-change lines that libubertooth raises alongside each map are \fBnote:\fR lines on standard error; see ubertooth(7), OUTPUT AND DIAGNOSTICS\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\. A bare \fBubertooth\-afh\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "GAPS IN A CAPTURE"
A capture that is losing packets says so\. The board holds captured packets in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next packet\. The first time it happens the capture warns \fBwarning: the board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost packets: one full queue can swallow several packets, and the board sends no number\. Nothing says which packets are missing, so a capture with gaps is complete only between them\. The board reports a gap on the first packet that gets through after it, so a capture that ends in the moment after a gap does not report that last one\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
\fBubertooth\-afh\fR does not stop on its own, so it normally exits 0 after being interrupted (Ctrl\-C)\. \fB\-h\fR also exits 0\.
.P
It exits 2 when the \fB\-t\fR detection phase received no packet from the piconet (see DETECTION AND MONITORING)\. The number of seconds in that message is the \fB\-t\fR value, not the time actually spent listening, which is shorter if the tool was interrupted during the phase\.
.P
It exits 1 without opening a device for a bad command line (a missing UAP or LAP, an option value out of range, or a word that is not an option) or when the piconet it tracks cannot be allocated (\fBerror: out of memory\fR), and when the selected Ubertooth is not found\. It also exits 1, with or without \fB\-r\fR, when the receive could not be started at all: a setup request the board refused, or a USB transfer that could not be set up\. One of those is the band sweep the whole band is watched through, without which the board stays on the channel it was left on and the map would hold that channel alone\.
.P
A run whose USB stream fails part\-way, with or without \fB\-r\fR, exits 1: the board stopped answering or dropped off the bus, so what it reported is whatever arrived before that\. A detection phase that lost its stream is never reported as a quiet piconet, so it exits 1 rather than 2\.
.P
A teardown the board refused exits 1 too, and this is one of the paths that reach it: the request that takes the board out of its capture mode was stalled, so the board is left in that mode and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. The map the run detected is still printed, and libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\. A quiet piconet is not reported as 2 in that case either\.

\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.

